  "cal.lift_matrix_hint": "Each row is a proposal to nudge some filter threshold. Δ columns show how KPIs would shift if we accepted that change (plus = better; for sl_rate, lower is better). Score is the overall usefulness rating: if it's ≥ MIN_LIFT_THRESHOLD, the change lands in the recommendations above and gets applied. Lower-scoring ideas sit in the queue until more data accumulates or the KPI starts pressing harder. Green row = above threshold.",
  "cal.lift_matrix_exit_hint": "Each row is a proposal to nudge an exit threshold (TP, SL, trailing). Δ columns show the expected impact on exit KPIs: fewer stop-outs, more take-profit hits, higher exit efficiency, less profit left on the table. Score is the overall usefulness rating: ≥ MIN_LIFT_THRESHOLD → the change gets applied; lower → it waits until more closed trades pile up.",
  "cal.lift_matrix_empty": "No proposals with score ≥ {threshold} yet. The matrix fills once enough closed trades accumulate to estimate the impact of a change. Check back later or lower MIN_LIFT_THRESHOLD in Settings.",
  "cal.tip_how_read": "How to read",
  "cal.tip_how_computed": "Where the numbers come from",
  "cal.tip_objective_body": "This bar shows which KPIs hurt the system most right now. The wider the column, the more weight that KPI carries in this cycle. Red = pressing hardest, needs fixing. Gray = within tolerance, the calibrator ignores it. These same weights feed into every proposal's score below.",
  "cal.tip_recs_body": "All proposals (entry + exit) in one list, ranked by score. Score is the usefulness rating — it combines how much a change would heal the painful KPIs with how reliable the underlying data is. Only proposals scoring ≥ MIN_LIFT_THRESHOLD are applied (noise protection). The rest sit in \"Filtered out\" below, waiting for more data or a lower threshold.",
  "cal.tip_attr_body": "How much money each filter saved and how much it took away. If a filter rejected a signal that would have lost — that's saved. If it rejected a winning one — that's lost. Net = saved − lost: positive means the filter earns its keep, negative means it's costing you profit.",
  "cal.tip_bayes_body": "How much you can trust each filter's stats. The more trades a filter has seen, the more reliable the verdict. ≥70% (stable) — enough data, decisions are well-grounded. 50–70% (researching) — still learning. <50% (uncertain) — too few samples, conclusions are noisy. Muted rows = filters with no data yet.",
  "cal.tip_closure_body": "How positions actually closed over the period. Lots of sl_fok / sl_emergency — stop-loss is set too tight, catching normal drawdowns. Lots of time_expiry — positions are sitting without resolution, TP/SL need to be pulled in. Avg PnL shows what each closure type typically yields.",
  "cal.tip_params_body": "Current — the live value. Default — the factory baseline. Yellow = the calibrator has already tweaked it. Left on table — profit we didn't take: the gap between the peak price the trade reached and what we actually locked in at exit.",

